;;; Producer.mon --- Existential producer calculus for sequence fusion

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.4.0
:keywords sequence producer existential fusion flat-map stateful rate normalization

;;; Commentary:

 Producer is the intensional machine calculus used to implement and fuse
 representation-changing sequence operations. It is not a second definition of
 sequence shape.

 A Producer existentially hides its private state:

                  Producer E A = exists S.
                    (S -> Step E S A) x S

 where

                  Step E S A = 1 + E S + A x E S.

 The hidden state is essential. Clients should depend on the yielded element and
 evaluation strategy, not on an implementation-specific chain of map/filter/take
 state wrappers. Existential packaging is the representation used by classical
 stream fusion and is precisely what lets arbitrarily many transforms remain one
 producer value while their internal state changes freely.

 ;;; Extensional Sequence versus Intensional Machine

 Sequence owns the visible coalgebra

                       1 + A x E X.

 Producer adds the silent Skip summand solely to describe internal progress:

                       1 + E X + A x E X.

 A finite prefix of Skip transitions is invisible when Producer is observed as a
 Sequence. An infinite Skip path is visible as divergence. The compiler may erase
 or rearrange hidden producer states only when visible observations, demanded
 divergence, evaluation order obligations, and quantitative usage are preserved.

 The Producer constructor is intentionally hidden from the public module
 surface. User code may construct producers through =producer=, but cannot unpack
 the existential state and make its representation part of ordinary semantics.
 This gives the optimizer freedom to normalize pipelines without turning private
 fusion state into a language-level observational commitment.

 ;;; Demand Discipline

 Every combinator below advances at most one raw input transition for each raw
 transition it constructs, except weak observation itself, which may remove a
 finite Skip prefix.

 Dormant continuations stay under E until their turn. Append never observes the
 right producer before the left producer stops. Zip observes left before right
 for each pair and keeps the unused continuation suspended. These ordering facts
 are semantic in the presence of divergence and therefore constrain fusion.

 For Lazy, optimization additionally satisfies demand refinement: a fused
 pipeline may demand less than the reference producer but never demand a
 continuation or retained callback earlier without a separate strictness proof.

 ;;; Fusion as Normalization

 Classical stream fusion relies on an existential state machine plus local
 Stop/Skip/Yield transformations. More recent complete-fusion work strengthens
 this perspective: pipeline compilation can be understood as normalization of a
 stream calculus to a state-machine normal form.

 Monad adopts the same architectural boundary without making Producer the
 extensional meaning of Sequence. Core supplies the machine and its equations;
 the compiler is responsible for specialization, state-product elimination,
 constructor elimination, loopification, and eventual normalization.

 For a statically closed producer P, the intended compiler normalization N has
 the following proof obligations:

                    observe (N P)  ~=d  observe P
                    N (N P)        =nf  N P

 where ~=d is visible demand equivalence and =nf means equality of the chosen
 first-order machine normal form up to alpha-renaming and state isomorphism.
 Normalization is required to terminate. Its output must contain no Core-level
 Producer, Step, View, higher-order transform closure, or intermediate tuple whose
 sole purpose was pipeline composition.

 These equations specify a target contract, not an executable =normalize=
 function in Core. The normalizer belongs to the compiler because its result is
 lower-level control flow, while the equations being normalized remain ordinary
 Core semantics.

 ;;; Expressiveness versus Complete Fusion

 The Step machine is extensionally universal for the regular one-output-at-a-time
 sequence interface. Zero-rate behavior is Skip; one-rate behavior is Yield; and
 many-rate behavior such as flat-map is represented by a product state that
 alternates between an outer Producer and the currently active inner Producer.

 This is enough to give every combinator below precise ordinary Core semantics.
 It is not, by itself, a theorem that compiling those Core values naively leaves
 no existential packages or closures. Flat-map is the critical example: its
 semantic state contains an existential inner Producer selected by a callback.

 Monad therefore distinguishes two claims:

   semantic fusion
     No intermediate Sequence is materialized; transforms compose as one
     Producer state machine.

   complete representation fusion
     After specialization of a statically known pipeline, Producer packages,
     Step constructors, closures, tuples, and transform wrappers are absent from
     generated code.

 The first claim follows from this module. The second is a compiler normalization
 obligation. The compiler must perform closure conversion/defunctionalization and
 normalize producer composition to a first-order state machine whenever the
 pipeline and its higher-order callbacks are statically available after
 specialization. A dynamically escaping Producer remains an ordinary first-class
 value and carries no zero-allocation promise.

 This boundary follows the lesson of complete-fusion work: nesting, differing
 rates, and zip require semantic normalization rather than optimism about a
 black-box inliner. Core supplies the reference calculus and equations; the
 compiler owns normalization to representation-free machine code.

 ;;; Research Basis

 Duncan Coutts, Roman Leshchinskiy, and Don Stewart, "Stream Fusion: From Lists
 to Streams to Nothing at All", ICFP 2007.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/1291151.1291199

 Ralf Hinze, Thomas Harper, and Daniel W. H. James, "Theory and Practice of
 Fusion", IFL 2010.
   https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-24276-2_2

 Oleg Kiselyov, Aggelos Biboudis, Nick Palladinos, and Yannis Smaragdakis,
 "Stream Fusion, to Completeness", POPL 2017.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/3009837.3009880

 Oleg Kiselyov, Tomoaki Kobayashi, and Nick Palladinos, "Complete Fusion for
 Stateful Streams: Equational Theory of Stateful Streams and Fusion as
 Normalization-by-Evaluation", arXiv:2412.15768, revised 2026.
   https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15768

 Tomoaki Kobayashi and Oleg Kiselyov, "Complete Stream Fusion for
 Software-Defined Radio", PEPM 2024.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/3635800.3636962

 The first two references motivate existential pull machines and coalgebraic
 fusion. The complete-fusion work demonstrates why nesting and rate differences
 must be normalized deliberately, and the SDR case study shows that stateful
 mapping and sliding windows need not become primitive sequence shapes.

;;; Code:

import Control.Evaluation
import Control.Applicative
import Data.Bool
import Data.Functor
import Data.Tuple

module Data.Sequence.Producer
  [Step Stop Skip Yield
   Producer producer observe-producer MapFilterResult Filtered Emitted
   filter-producer take-producer drop-producer
   take-while-producer drop-while-producer
   append-producer zip-producer zip-with-producer
   flat-map-producer map-accumulate-producer map-filter-accumulate-producer
   scan-producer enumerate-producer intersperse-producer]


;;; Existential Machine


data Step e s a
  = Stop
  | Skip (e s)
  | Yield a (e s)


instance Functor e => Bifunctor (Step e)
  bimap _ _ Stop -> Stop

  bimap map-state _ [Skip next] ->
    Skip (map map-state next)

  bimap map-state map-element [Yield value next] ->
    Yield (map-element value) (map map-state next)


;; S is existential: it occurs in the constructor arguments but not in the
;; Producer result. The compiler must preserve this abstraction boundary.
data Producer :: (Type -> Type) -> Type -> Type
  ProducerState : (s -> Step e s a) -> s -> Producer e a


define producer :: (s -> Step e s a) -> s -> Producer e a
  transition initial -> ProducerState transition initial


define map-step-element :: Functor e => (a -> b) -> Step e s a -> Step e s b
  _ Stop -> Stop
  _ [Skip next] -> Skip next
  f [Yield value next] -> Yield (f value) next


define map-step-state :: Functor e => (s -> t) -> Step e s a -> Step e t a
  _ Stop -> Stop
  f [Skip next] -> Skip (map f next)
  f [Yield value next] -> Yield value (map f next)


define map-producer-transition :: Functor e =>
  (a -> b) -> (s -> Step e s a) -> s -> Step e s b
  f transition state -> map-step-element f (transition state)


instance Functor e => Functor (Producer e)
  map f [ProducerState transition state] ->
    ProducerState (map-producer-transition f transition) state


;;; Weak Observation


;; Perform one transition while immediately repackaging the private state. The
;; existential S never escapes this eliminator: subsequent code works with the
;; uniform state type Producer e a.
define advance-producer :: Functor e =>
  Producer e a -> Step e (Producer e a) a
  [ProducerState transition state] ->
    map-step-state (lambda (next) (ProducerState transition next)) (transition state)


data Observation e a
  = NoObservation Bool
  | Observed a (e (Producer e a))


define observation-result :: Observation e a -> (a, e (Producer e a))?
  [NoObservation _] -> nil
  [Observed value next] -> (value, next)


define demand-producer :: Evaluation e => e (Producer e a) -> Producer e a
  next -> demand next


define observe-machine-step :: Evaluation e => Step e (Producer e a) a -> Observation e a
  [Skip next] -> observe-machine (demand-producer next)
  [Yield value next] -> Observed value next
  _ -> NoObservation False


define observe-machine :: Evaluation e => Producer e a -> Observation e a
  source -> observe-machine-step (advance-producer source)


define observe-producer :: Evaluation e =>
  Producer e a -> (a, e (Producer e a))?
  source -> observation-result (observe-machine source)


;;; Local Transformations


define filter-step :: (a -> Bool) -> Step e s a -> Step e s a
  _ Stop -> Stop
  _ [Skip next] -> Skip next
  predicate [Yield value next]
    | predicate value -> Yield value next
    | otherwise       -> Skip next


define filter-transition :: (a -> Bool) -> (s -> Step e s a) -> s -> Step e s a
  predicate transition state -> filter-step predicate (transition state)


define filter-producer :: (a -> Bool) -> Producer e a -> Producer e a
  predicate [ProducerState transition state] ->
    ProducerState (filter-transition predicate transition) state


;; Take counts Yield transitions. Skip never consumes the visible budget.
data TakeState e s a
  = TakeState Int (s -> Step e s a) s


define take-next :: Functor e =>
  Int -> (s -> Step e s a) -> Step e s a -> Step e (TakeState e s a) a
  _ _ Stop -> Stop

  amount transition [Skip next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (state) (TakeState amount transition state)) next)

  amount transition [Yield value next] ->
    Yield value
      (map (lambda (state) (TakeState (amount - 1) transition state)) next)


define take-transition :: Functor e =>
  TakeState e s a -> Step e (TakeState e s a) a
  [TakeState amount _ _]
    | amount <= 0 -> Stop

  [TakeState amount transition state] ->
    take-next amount transition (transition state)


define take-producer :: Functor e =>
  Int -> Producer e a -> Producer e a
  amount [ProducerState transition state] ->
    ProducerState take-transition (TakeState amount transition state)


;; Drop turns the first N Yield transitions into silent progress.
data DropState e s a
  = DropState Int (s -> Step e s a) s


define pass-drop-step :: Functor e =>
  (s -> Step e s a) -> Step e s a -> Step e (DropState e s a) a
  transition value ->
    map-step-state (lambda (state) (DropState 0 transition state)) value


define drop-next :: Functor e =>
  Int -> (s -> Step e s a) -> Step e s a -> Step e (DropState e s a) a
  _ _ Stop -> Stop

  amount transition [Skip next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (state) (DropState amount transition state)) next)

  amount transition [Yield _ next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (state) (DropState (amount - 1) transition state)) next)


define drop-transition :: Functor e =>
  DropState e s a -> Step e (DropState e s a) a
  [DropState amount transition state]
    | amount <= 0 -> pass-drop-step transition (transition state)

  [DropState amount transition state] ->
    drop-next amount transition (transition state)


define drop-producer :: Functor e =>
  Int -> Producer e a -> Producer e a
  amount [ProducerState transition state] ->
    ProducerState drop-transition (DropState amount transition state)


;; TakeWhile terminates on the first rejected Yield without demanding its tail.
data TakeWhileState e s a
  = TakeWhileState (a -> Bool) (s -> Step e s a) s


define take-while-next :: Functor e =>
  (a -> Bool) -> (s -> Step e s a) -> Step e s a
  -> Step e (TakeWhileState e s a) a
  _ _ Stop -> Stop

  predicate transition [Skip next] ->
    Skip
      (map (lambda (state) (TakeWhileState predicate transition state)) next)

  predicate transition [Yield value next]
    | predicate value ->
        Yield value
          (map (lambda (state) (TakeWhileState predicate transition state)) next)
    | otherwise -> Stop


define take-while-transition :: Functor e =>
  TakeWhileState e s a -> Step e (TakeWhileState e s a) a
  [TakeWhileState predicate transition state] ->
    take-while-next predicate transition (transition state)


define take-while-producer :: Functor e =>
  (a -> Bool) -> Producer e a -> Producer e a
  predicate [ProducerState transition state] ->
    ProducerState take-while-transition
      (TakeWhileState predicate transition state)


;; DropWhile has a dropping phase and a transition-transparent passing phase.
data DropWhileState e s a
  = Dropping (a -> Bool) (s -> Step e s a) s
  | Passing (s -> Step e s a) s


define passing-next :: Functor e =>
  (s -> Step e s a) -> Step e s a -> Step e (DropWhileState e s a) a
  transition value ->
    map-step-state (lambda (state) (Passing transition state)) value


define dropping-next :: Functor e =>
  (a -> Bool) -> (s -> Step e s a) -> Step e s a
  -> Step e (DropWhileState e s a) a
  _ _ Stop -> Stop

  predicate transition [Skip next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (state) (Dropping predicate transition state)) next)

  predicate transition [Yield value next]
    | predicate value ->
        Skip (map (lambda (state) (Dropping predicate transition state)) next)
    | otherwise ->
        Yield value (map (lambda (state) (Passing transition state)) next)


define drop-while-transition :: Functor e =>
  DropWhileState e s a -> Step e (DropWhileState e s a) a
  [Passing transition state] ->
    passing-next transition (transition state)

  [Dropping predicate transition state] ->
    dropping-next predicate transition (transition state)


define drop-while-producer :: Functor e =>
  (a -> Bool) -> Producer e a -> Producer e a
  predicate [ProducerState transition state] ->
    ProducerState drop-while-transition
      (Dropping predicate transition state)


;;; Ordered Composition


;; Append never observes RIGHT before LEFT terminates.
data AppendState e l r a
  = AppendLeft (l -> Step e l a) l (r -> Step e r a) r
  | AppendRight (r -> Step e r a) r


define append-right-next :: Functor e =>
  (r -> Step e r a) -> Step e r a -> Step e (AppendState e l r a) a
  transition value ->
    map-step-state (lambda (state) (AppendRight transition state)) value


define append-left-next :: Evaluation e =>
  (l -> Step e l a) -> (r -> Step e r a) -> r -> Step e l a
  -> Step e (AppendState e l r a) a
  _ right-transition right-state Stop ->
    Skip (defer (lambda (_) (AppendRight right-transition right-state)))

  left-transition right-transition right-state [Skip next] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (state)
          (AppendLeft left-transition state right-transition right-state))
        next)

  left-transition right-transition right-state [Yield value next] ->
    Yield value
      (map
        (lambda (state)
          (AppendLeft left-transition state right-transition right-state))
        next)


define append-transition :: Evaluation e =>
  AppendState e l r a -> Step e (AppendState e l r a) a
  [AppendRight transition state] ->
    append-right-next transition (transition state)

  [AppendLeft left-transition left-state right-transition right-state] ->
    append-left-next left-transition right-transition right-state
      (left-transition left-state)


define append-producer :: Evaluation e =>
  Producer e a -> Producer e a -> Producer e a
  [ProducerState left-transition left-state]
  [ProducerState right-transition right-state] ->
    ProducerState append-transition
      (AppendLeft left-transition left-state right-transition right-state)


;; Zip is left-to-right within each output pair. RIGHT is untouched until LEFT
;; has yielded the value for that pair.
data ZipState e l r a b
  = NeedLeft (l -> Step e l a) l (r -> Step e r b) r
  | NeedRight a (e l) (l -> Step e l a) (r -> Step e r b) r


define zip-left-next :: Evaluation e =>
  (l -> Step e l a) -> (r -> Step e r b) -> r -> Step e l a
  -> Step e (ZipState e l r a b) (a, b)
  _ _ _ Stop -> Stop

  left-transition right-transition right-state [Skip next] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (state)
          (NeedLeft left-transition state right-transition right-state))
        next)

  left-transition right-transition right-state [Yield value next] ->
    Skip
      (defer
        (lambda (_)
          (NeedRight value next left-transition right-transition right-state)))


define zip-right-next :: Evaluation e =>
  a -> e l -> (l -> Step e l a) -> (r -> Step e r b) -> Step e r b
  -> Step e (ZipState e l r a b) (a, b)
  _ _ _ _ Stop -> Stop

  left-value left-next left-transition right-transition [Skip next] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (state)
          (NeedRight left-value left-next left-transition right-transition state))
        next)

  left-value left-next left-transition right-transition [Yield right-value next] ->
    Yield (left-value, right-value)
      (defer
        (lambda (_)
          (NeedLeft left-transition (demand left-next)
            right-transition (demand next))))


define zip-transition :: Evaluation e =>
  ZipState e l r a b -> Step e (ZipState e l r a b) (a, b)
  [NeedLeft left-transition left-state right-transition right-state] ->
    zip-left-next left-transition right-transition right-state
      (left-transition left-state)

  [NeedRight left-value left-next left-transition right-transition right-state] ->
    zip-right-next left-value left-next left-transition right-transition
      (right-transition right-state)


define zip-producer :: Evaluation e =>
  Producer e a -> Producer e b -> Producer e (a, b)
  [ProducerState left-transition left-state]
  [ProducerState right-transition right-state] ->
    ProducerState zip-transition
      (NeedLeft left-transition left-state right-transition right-state)


define zip-with-producer :: Evaluation e =>
  (a -> b -> c) -> Producer e a -> Producer e b -> Producer e c
  combine left right ->
    map (uncurry combine) (zip-producer left right)


;;; Nested and Stateful Composition


;; FlatMap changes production rate: one outer element may produce zero, one, or
;; arbitrarily many inner elements. OUTER-REST stays suspended while INNER is
;; live, so an infinite inner producer never demands an unreachable outer tail.
data FlatMapState e a b
  = NeedOuter (a -> Producer e b) (Producer e a)
  | NeedInner (a -> Producer e b) (e (Producer e a)) (Producer e b)


define flat-map-outer-next :: Evaluation e =>
  (a -> Producer e b) -> Step e (Producer e a) a
  -> Step e (FlatMapState e a b) b
  _ Stop -> Stop

  expand [Skip outer-next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (next) (NeedOuter expand next)) outer-next)

  expand [Yield value outer-next] ->
    Skip
      (defer
        (lambda (_)
          (NeedInner expand outer-next (expand value))))


define flat-map-inner-next :: Evaluation e =>
  (a -> Producer e b) -> e (Producer e a) -> Step e (Producer e b) b
  -> Step e (FlatMapState e a b) b
  expand outer-next Stop ->
    Skip (map (lambda (next) (NeedOuter expand next)) outer-next)

  expand outer-next [Skip inner-next] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (next) (NeedInner expand outer-next next))
        inner-next)

  expand outer-next [Yield value inner-next] ->
    Yield value
      (map
        (lambda (next) (NeedInner expand outer-next next))
        inner-next)


define flat-map-transition :: Evaluation e =>
  FlatMapState e a b -> Step e (FlatMapState e a b) b
  [NeedOuter expand outer] ->
    flat-map-outer-next expand (advance-producer outer)

  [NeedInner expand outer-next inner] ->
    flat-map-inner-next expand outer-next (advance-producer inner)


define flat-map-producer :: Evaluation e =>
  (a -> Producer e b) -> Producer e a -> Producer e b
  expand outer ->
    ProducerState flat-map-transition (NeedOuter expand outer)


;; map-accumulate is the general one-output-per-Yield stateful map. Source Skip
;; transitions never update the accumulator.
data MapAccumulateState e state a b
  = MapAccumulateState (state -> a -> (state, b)) state (Producer e a)


define map-accumulate-next :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> (state, b)) -> state -> Step e (Producer e a) a
  -> Step e (MapAccumulateState e state a b) b
  _ _ Stop -> Stop

  combine accumulator [Skip next] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (source)
          (MapAccumulateState combine accumulator source))
        next)

  combine accumulator [Yield value next] ->
    (map-accumulate-yield combine next (combine accumulator value))


define map-accumulate-yield :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> (state, b)) -> e (Producer e a) -> (state, b)
  -> Step e (MapAccumulateState e state a b) b
  combine next result ->
    Yield (snd result) (map (lambda (source) (MapAccumulateState combine (fst result) source)) next)


define map-accumulate-transition :: Functor e =>
  MapAccumulateState e state a b
  -> Step e (MapAccumulateState e state a b) b
  [MapAccumulateState combine accumulator source] ->
    map-accumulate-next combine accumulator (advance-producer source)


define map-accumulate-producer :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> (state, b)) -> state -> Producer e a -> Producer e b
  combine initial source ->
    ProducerState map-accumulate-transition (MapAccumulateState combine initial source)


;; map-filter-accumulate is the general stateful zero-or-one-rate transform.
;; Returning nil advances state without producing a visible element.
data MapFilterResult state b
  = Filtered state
  | Emitted state b


data MapFilterAccumulateState e state a b
  = MapFilterAccumulateState (state -> a -> MapFilterResult state b) state (Producer e a)


define map-filter-result :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> MapFilterResult state b) -> e (Producer e a) -> MapFilterResult state b
  -> Step e (MapFilterAccumulateState e state a b) b
  combine next [Filtered following] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (source)
          (MapFilterAccumulateState combine following source))
        next)

  combine next [Emitted following output] ->
    Yield output
      (map
        (lambda (source)
          (MapFilterAccumulateState combine following source))
        next)


define map-filter-accumulate-next :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> MapFilterResult state b) -> state -> Step e (Producer e a) a
  -> Step e (MapFilterAccumulateState e state a b) b
  _ _ Stop -> Stop

  combine accumulator [Skip next] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (source)
          (MapFilterAccumulateState combine accumulator source))
        next)

  combine accumulator [Yield value next] ->
    map-filter-result combine next (combine accumulator value)


define map-filter-accumulate-transition :: Functor e =>
  MapFilterAccumulateState e state a b
  -> Step e (MapFilterAccumulateState e state a b) b
  [MapFilterAccumulateState combine accumulator source] ->
    map-filter-accumulate-next combine accumulator (advance-producer source)


define map-filter-accumulate-producer :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> MapFilterResult state b) -> state -> Producer e a -> Producer e b
  combine initial source ->
    ProducerState map-filter-accumulate-transition (MapFilterAccumulateState combine initial source)


;; Scan emits the seed once, then every successive accumulator.
data ScanState e state a
  = ScanSeed (state -> a -> state) state (Producer e a)
  | ScanMore (state -> a -> state) state (Producer e a)


define scan-more-next :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> state) -> state -> Step e (Producer e a) a
  -> Step e (ScanState e state a) state
  _ _ Stop -> Stop

  combine accumulator [Skip next] ->
    Skip
      (map
        (lambda (source)
          (ScanMore combine accumulator source))
        next)

  combine accumulator [Yield value next] ->
    (scan-yield combine next (combine accumulator value))


define scan-yield :: Functor e =>
  (state -> a -> state) -> e (Producer e a) -> state
  -> Step e (ScanState e state a) state
  combine next following ->
    Yield following (map (lambda (source) (ScanMore combine following source)) next)


define scan-transition :: Evaluation e =>
  ScanState e state a -> Step e (ScanState e state a) state
  [ScanSeed combine initial source] ->
    Yield initial
      (defer
        (lambda (_)
          (ScanMore combine initial source)))

  [ScanMore combine accumulator source] ->
    scan-more-next combine accumulator (advance-producer source)


define scan-producer :: Evaluation e =>
  (state -> a -> state) -> state -> Producer e a -> Producer e state
  combine initial source ->
    ProducerState scan-transition (ScanSeed combine initial source)


data EnumerateState e a
  = EnumerateState Int (Producer e a)


define enumerate-next :: Functor e =>
  Int -> Step e (Producer e a) a -> Step e (EnumerateState e a) (Int, a)
  _ Stop -> Stop
  index [Skip next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (source) (EnumerateState index source)) next)
  index [Yield value next] ->
    Yield (index, value)
      (map (lambda (source) (EnumerateState (+ index 1) source)) next)


define enumerate-transition :: Functor e =>
  EnumerateState e a -> Step e (EnumerateState e a) (Int, a)
  [EnumerateState index source] ->
    enumerate-next index (advance-producer source)


define enumerate-producer :: Functor e =>
  Producer e a -> Producer e (Int, a)
  source -> ProducerState enumerate-transition (EnumerateState 0 source)


;; Intersperse has a buffered phase because a separator is emitted only after a
;; following source value is known to exist. No source continuation is demanded
;; merely to emit the buffered value.
data IntersperseState e a
  = FirstValue a (Producer e a)
  | NeedValue a (Producer e a)
  | BufferedValue a a (e (Producer e a))


define first-value-next :: Functor e =>
  a -> Step e (Producer e a) a -> Step e (IntersperseState e a) a
  _ Stop -> Stop

  separator [Skip next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (source) (FirstValue separator source)) next)

  separator [Yield value next] ->
    Yield value
      (map (lambda (source) (NeedValue separator source)) next)


define need-value-next :: Evaluation e =>
  a -> Step e (Producer e a) a -> Step e (IntersperseState e a) a
  _ Stop -> Stop

  separator [Skip next] ->
    Skip (map (lambda (source) (NeedValue separator source)) next)

  separator [Yield value next] ->
    Yield separator
      (defer
        (lambda (_)
          (BufferedValue separator value next)))


define intersperse-transition :: Evaluation e =>
  IntersperseState e a -> Step e (IntersperseState e a) a
  [FirstValue separator source] ->
    first-value-next separator (advance-producer source)

  [NeedValue separator source] ->
    need-value-next separator (advance-producer source)

  [BufferedValue separator value next] ->
    Yield value
      (map (lambda (source) (NeedValue separator source)) next)


define intersperse-producer :: Evaluation e =>
  a -> Producer e a -> Producer e a
  separator source ->
    ProducerState intersperse-transition (FirstValue separator source)
